---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CaseStudyHeader from "../../components/CaseStudyHeader.astro";
import InteractiveSceneGenerator from "../../islands/InteractiveSceneGenerator";
---

<Layout
  title="Synthwave Scene Generator"
  description="A procedural canvas engine generating retro-neon sunset scenes, with a seeded RNG and three curated palettes."
>
  <CaseStudyHeader
    title="Synthwave Scene Generator"
    summary="A procedural canvas engine that draws a retro-neon sunset scene — the exact image I described unprompted when asked what I wanted this site to look like. It's never the same scene twice."
    tags={["TypeScript", "Canvas 2D API", "Vite", "No framework"]}
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">Try it</h2>
    <p class="prose mt-2 text-[var(--text-dim)]">
      This is the actual project, running live — not a screenshot. Pick a palette, drag the slider from dusk to
      night, and hit Randomize for a new layout.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <InteractiveSceneGenerator client:visible />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-10">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">Technical approach</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-3 text-[var(--text-dim)]">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white">Seeded procedural generation.</strong> A <code class="mono">mulberry32</code> pseudo-random
        generator means every scene is fully reproducible from its seed — the mountains, star field, and whether it
        draws mountains or a small gas-station structure are all deterministic given that seed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white">Layout and rendering are deliberately separate.</strong> The composition (mountain
        shape, star positions, silhouette choice) generates once per seed. Palette and time-of-day changes re-render
        every frame without touching that layout — that's what makes the slider feel instant instead of janky.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white">Curated, not infinite, color choice.</strong> Three hand-picked palettes instead of
        a full color wheel — a deliberate constraint, since infinite choice is worse design than a few good ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white">No framework.</strong> This is one interactive canvas with no routing or shared
        app state — a UI framework would have been pure overhead. Plain TypeScript against the Canvas 2D API.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-white">What this demonstrates</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--text-dim)]">
      Getting the retro "scanline sun" cutout and the perspective grid to read correctly mattered more than any
      individual shape — those two details are what separate this from just being a dark gradient with a circle on
      it. The hero on this site's homepage is the same code, running with a fixed palette and a slow ambient
      dusk-to-night drift instead of manual controls.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
